{# DECIDED: loki-callers sits in the observability namespace and admits homelab-mcp and
   airflow-task from the homelab namespace, so it stays bespoke.
   The callers model (`netpol_from` / `netpol_fences`, macro in
   k8s/templates/networkpolicy.yml.j2) renders one TCP port admitting same-namespace `app:`
   peers, and its header rules out growing it to absorb another shape.
#}
---
# THIS loki (the observability one) has two cross-namespace, in-cluster callers. homelab-mcp
# reads it via CLAUDE_LOKI_URL, not the plain LOKI_URL homelab-mcp also holds, which points at
# the different loki-homelab service in {{ k8s_namespace }}. Airflow's task pods
# (`app: airflow-task`) read its Claude Code events for the warehouse's claude_decisions_rollup.
# monitor-bridge does not call THIS loki: its
# LOKI_URL, check_loki_ingestion and check_loki_reachable all target loki-homelab.
#
# loki itself is ALSO read on host loopback by `otelq` (a workstation host process — see
# observability/CLAUDE.md), covered the same way as tempo/otel-collector below, not by this file.
# The other four need no per-workload policy, each for a different reason — don't collapse them
# into one mechanism:
#   - tempo, otel-collector: loopback-hostPort callers only, admitted by the node-CIDR `ipBlock`
#     peer (`netpol_baseline_obs_node_cidrs`, networkpolicy-observability.yaml.j2), which carries
#     no `ports` restriction — trimming that list to "nameable" entries breaks otelq silently.
#   - grafana: Traefik only, admitted by that same file's explicit traefik
#     namespaceSelector+podSelector peer — unrelated to the ipBlock/CIDR list above.
#   - kube-state-metrics: no external caller at all; prometheus scrapes it from inside the
#     namespace, covered by the plain `podSelector: {}` peer.
#
# INGRESS ONLY, same as the rest of this role: an Egress policy selects pods correctly on this
# cluster and blocks nothing. Writing one would read like a control in the repo and do nothing in
# the cluster.
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: loki-callers
  namespace: {{ k8s_observability_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: loki
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
{# `| bool`, matching `netpol_baseline_probe_active` in defaults/main.yml: a STRING value like
   `'false'` is truthy to bare Jinja and False to that list, which picks the verify Job's legs,
   so without `| bool` the two sides disagree and this policy would fence loki with no probe to
   catch it. #}
{% if netpol_baseline_obs_enforced | bool %}
    - from:
        # namespaceSelector and podSelector are SIBLING KEYS in ONE list item, so they AND
        # together: the listed apps, but only inside {{ k8s_namespace }}. As two separate list
        # items they would OR, admitting the entire {{ k8s_namespace }} namespace (or any pod
        # anywhere carrying a listed label) rather than just these callers.
        - namespaceSelector:
            matchLabels:
              kubernetes.io/metadata.name: {{ k8s_namespace }}
          podSelector:
            matchExpressions:
              - key: app
                operator: In
                values: [airflow-task, homelab-mcp]
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: 3100
{% else %}
    # OFF. One empty rule admits everything. `ingress: []` would be the exact opposite and deny
    # everything, which is the easiest thing in this file to get backwards.
    - {}
{% endif %}
